    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is true regarding "Capacity" vs. "Competence"?', '["They are the same thing", "Capacity is a clinical determination made by a physician; Competence is a legal determination made by a judge", "Competence is for minors; Capacity is for adults", "Physicians determine competence; Judges determine capacity"]', 1, 'Capacity is the clinical assessment of a person''s ability to make a specific decision. Competence is the legal status determined by a court.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with dementia is being asked to consent to a complex, high-risk neurosurgery. They understand the basics but cannot reason through the long-term trade-offs. The physician determines they lack capacity for *this* decision. Does this mean they lack capacity for *all* decisions?', '["Yes, capacity is all-or-nothing", "No, capacity is decision-specific; they might still have capacity to choose their dinner or consent to a simple blood draw", "Only if they have a guardian", "Only if they are over 80"]', 1, 'Capacity is a sliding scale. The more complex or risky the decision, the higher the "threshold" of capacity required.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is delirious in the morning but clear-headed and interactive in the afternoon. When should the physician perform the capacity assessment for a non-urgent procedure?', '["In the morning, to be safe", "In the afternoon, when the patient is at their baseline and most likely to demonstrate capacity", "It doesn''t matter, capacity must be permanent", "Never, delirious patients always lack capacity"]', 1, 'Physicians should optimize the patient''s condition (e.g., waiting for delirium to clear or for "sundowning" to resolve) before assessing capacity for non-urgent decisions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is a common clinical tool used specifically to help structure a decision-making capacity assessment?', '["The GCS Scale", "The MacArthur Competence Assessment Tool (MacCAT)", "The APGAR Score", "The NYHA Classification"]', 1, 'While not always required, tools like the MacCAT-T provide a structured way to assess the four elements of capacity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with schizophrenia is hearing voices but currently understands that their leg is broken and that they need surgery to walk again. They are not delusional about the surgery itself. Do they have capacity?', '["No, psychotic patients never have capacity", "Yes, the presence of mental illness does not automatically negate capacity if the illness does not impair the specific decision at hand", "Only if they are on medication", "Only if a judge says so"]', 1, 'Mental illness is a risk factor for incapacity, but it is not a diagnosis of incapacity. The assessment must focus on the decision-making process for the specific clinical problem.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'If a patient is unable to "Reason" through a decision, it means they cannot:', '["Hear the physician", "Compare the risks and benefits to reach a logical conclusion that aligns with their own values", "Understand the English language", "State their choice clearly"]', 1, 'Reasoning involves the logical manipulation of the information provided to arrive at a choice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient refuses a life-saving blood transfusion. When asked why, they say: "I just don''t want it, I don''t have to explain myself." Does this patient demonstrate capacity?', '["Yes, autonomy means no explanations are needed", "No, because the ''Reasoning'' element requires that the patient be able to explain the logic behind their choice", "Yes, as long as they are awake", "None of the above"]', 1, 'A core element of capacity is the ability to provide a rational reason for the choice (even if the reason is based on "irrational" values, the logic must be present).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following medical conditions is a "Transient" cause of incapacity?', '["Severe Delirium due to infection", "Acute Alcohol Intoxication", "Post-ictal state after a seizure", "All of the above"]', 3, 'Transient conditions should be managed or allowed to resolve before a definitive capacity determination is made for non-emergencies.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe depression refuses treatment because they "deserve to die." Which element of capacity is primarily impaired?', '["Understanding", "Communication", "Appreciation (due to the impact of the mood disorder on their perception of their own value and future)", "None of the above"]', 2, 'Severe depression can cloud a patient''s "Appreciation" of the consequences, as they may view death not as a risk but as a desired or deserved outcome of the illness.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When is a formal "Psychiatric Consultation" *legally* required for a capacity assessment?', '["Always, for every patient", "Only when the physician performing the procedure is unsure or if a mental health condition is the primary cause of the potential impairment", "Only for minors", "Only for inmates"]', 1, 'Any physician can (and should) perform initial capacity assessments. Psychiatry is called for assistance in complex or disputed cases.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient lacks capacity and has no advance directives. The family disagrees on the plan. The physician should:', '["Follow the loudest person", "Consult the hospital ethics committee to help resolve the conflict and determine the best path forward", "Do nothing", "Call the police"]', 1, 'Ethics committees provide mediation and guidance in cases of surrogate disagreement or complex capacity issues.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is NOT one of the "Sliding Scale" factors that increases the required level of capacity?', '["The complexity of the procedure", "The risk of the procedure", "The patient''s income", "The gravity of the consequences of the decision"]', 2, 'The threshold for capacity is based on the clinical stakes, not the patient''s socioeconomic status.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is "Aphasic" (unable to speak) but can communicate "Yes" or "No" by blinking or squeezing a hand consistently. Can they have capacity?', '["No, they must be able to speak", "Yes, as long as they can consistently and clearly communicate a choice through any reliable means", "Only if they can write", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Communication does not require speech; it only requires a reliable method of expressing a preference.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old patient with mild cognitive impairment wants to refuse a risky surgery. They understand they might die without it and say they have "lived a full life and are ready." Does this reflect a lack of capacity?', '["Yes, it''s a suicidal thought", "No, this reflects a choice based on personal values (Quality of Life vs. Quantity of Life) and may be a perfectly capacitated decision", "Only if they have a Living Will", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Values-based decisions that prioritize comfort over longevity are common and often capacitated.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'If a patient is "Incapacitated" and an emergency surgery is needed, but the spouse says: "He always told me he''d never want his chest opened." The surgeon should:', '["Proceed anyway under implied consent", "Respect the spouse''s report of the patient''s prior wishes (Substituted Judgment) and find a less invasive option if possible", "Tell the spouse they are wrong", "None of the above"]', 1, 'A reliable surrogate''s report of the patient''s specific prior wishes overrides the general "implied consent" of an emergency.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following describes "Prospective Capacity"?', '["The ability to see the future", "Assessing if a patient currently has capacity to make decisions that will apply to a future state (e.g., signing a DPOA)", "Capacity for someone who is currently dead", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Advance directives are made when a patient has "prospective capacity" to plan for future incapacity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is intoxicated with a blood alcohol level of 0.25. They want to leave the hospital "Against Medical Advice" (AMA). The physician should:', '["Let them go, it''s their right", "Temporarily detain them (under ''Implied Consent'' for safety) until they are sober enough to demonstrate capacity to understand the risks of leaving", "Call their parents", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Acute intoxication is a state of temporary incapacity. Letting an intoxicated, unstable patient leave can be considered medical neglect/malpractice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The "Mini-Mental State Exam" (MMSE) is used to:', '["Determine capacity", "Screen for cognitive impairment (like dementia), which is a risk factor for but NOT a diagnosis of incapacity", "Diagnose depression", "Measure intelligence"]', 1, 'The MMSE (or MoCA) is a screening tool. A low score suggests the need for a deeper capacity assessment, but it is not the assessment itself.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient refuses to even *listen* to the physician explain the risks of a procedure. Does this patient have capacity?', '["Yes, they are exercising their right to silence", "No, because the ''Understanding'' element of capacity cannot be met if the patient refuses to receive the information", "Only if they have a lawyer", "None of the above"]', 1, 'To have capacity for a decision, one must first be willing and able to receive the information relevant to that decision.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient says "The aliens told me this surgery is a trap." This is a failure of which capacity element?', '["Communication", "Reasoning (the logic is based on a delusion rather than reality)", "Comprehension", "None of the above"]', 1, 'While the patient "communicated," their reasoning is impaired by a psychotic process.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Can a physician override a capacitated patient''s refusal of treatment because the physician thinks the patient is "Depressed"?', '["Yes, depression always negates capacity", "No, not unless the depression is so severe that it specifically impairs the patient''s ''Appreciation'' or ''Reasoning'' for that specific medical decision", "Only if the patient is a woman", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Depression is a common condition. It only negates capacity if it creates a "cognitive distortion" that prevents the patient from rationally assessing the medical facts.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is in "Intensive Care" and is on a ventilator. They are awake and follow commands. To assess capacity for a decision, the physician should:', '["Wait until they are off the ventilator", "Use communication boards, letter charts, or other aids to facilitate the patient''s ability to express their choice and reason", "Assume they lack capacity", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Mechanical ventilation does not equal incapacity. Every effort must be made to communicate with the patient.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is true about "Capacity" in patients with Intellectual Disabilities?', '["They always lack capacity", "The assessment must be tailored to their level of understanding, often using simpler language and visual aids to determine if they can meet the basic elements of capacity", "They only have capacity if they are married", "None of the above"]', 1, 'The standard for capacity (Understanding, Appreciation, Reasoning, Communication) remains the same, but the *delivery* of information must be adapted to the patient.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a brain tumor has "Expressive Aphasia" (they know what they want to say but the words come out wrong). How should capacity be assessed?', '["Assume they lack capacity", "Use non-verbal methods (pointing to pictures, squeezing hands) and involve a speech-language pathologist to help bridge the communication gap", "Wait for the tumor to shrink", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Expressive aphasia is a "Communication" barrier, not necessarily a "Reasoning" or "Understanding" barrier.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient has "Sundowning" (worsening confusion in the evening). A non-urgent surgical consent should be obtained:', '["At 10 PM", "At 9 AM, when the patient is at their peak cognitive performance", "Only on weekends", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Timing the assessment to the patient''s "best" hours is a key part of fair capacity evaluation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old man with end-stage liver disease is "Encephalopathic" (confused due to toxins). He refuses a procedure. The physician should:', '["Respect the refusal", "Determine that the encephalopathy is causing acute incapacity and look to a surrogate for the decision", "Let him die", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Encephalopathy is a physiological cause of temporary incapacity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is an "Objective" sign of incapacity during an interview?', '["The patient repeats the physician''s words without understanding", "The patient changes their mind every 30 seconds (instability of choice)", "The patient cannot state the primary purpose of the surgery after it has been explained several times", "All of the above"]', 3, 'Inconsistency and failure to comprehend core facts are clear indicators of a lack of capacity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A "Psychiatric Advance Directive" (PAD) allows a patient with a chronic mental illness to:', '["Plan for their funeral", "Appoint a proxy and state their treatment preferences for future periods of psychiatric crisis when they may lack capacity", "Sue their psychiatrist", "None of the above"]', 1, 'PADs are useful for patients with conditions like Bipolar Disorder or Schizophrenia who have predictable periods of incapacity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Is "Capacity" a permanent label?', '["Yes, once a doctor says you lack it, it''s for life", "No, it is a point-in-time assessment. A patient can lack capacity today (due to a UTI) and have it tomorrow (after the UTI is treated)", "Only for elderly people", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Capacity is dynamic and should be re-evaluated as the patient''s clinical status changes.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient "Lacks Capacity" but is refusing a life-saving treatment. The family also refuses. The physician should:', '["Listen to the family", "Consult legal counsel and potentially seek a ''Court-Ordered'' treatment if the refusal is deemed to be in clear violation of the patient''s best interests (especially if the surrogate is not acting in good faith)", "Do nothing", "None of the above"]', 1, 'While surrogates have authority, that authority is not absolute if their decisions are clearly harmful and unsupported by the patient''s prior wishes.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The "Best Interests Standard" is often criticized because:', '["It is too clear", "It is subjective and depends on the values of the person (doctor or surrogate) determining what is ''best", "It ignores the law", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Determining what is "best" is value-laden. This is why "Substituted Judgment" (using the patient''s own values) is preferred whenever possible.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with capacity chooses a treatment that has a lower success rate but allows them to stay at home rather than in a hospital. This decision is:', '["Evidence of incapacity", "A valid exercise of autonomy that the physician should respect", "A sign of depression", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Patients are allowed to value "Quality of Life" or "Personal Convenience" over "Maximum Medical Benefit."'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which element of capacity is most likely impaired in a patient with "Anosognosia" (denial of illness)?', '["Communication", "Appreciation (they cannot appreciate that they are sick and therefore cannot appreciate the consequences of the decision)", "Understanding", "None of the above"]', 1, 'If a patient doesn''t believe they have the condition, they cannot appreciate the relevance of the treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient has a "Guardian" for their finances but not for their medical care. Can they provide medical consent?', '["No, guardianships are all-encompassing", "Yes, ''Limited Guardianship'' allows a person to retain autonomy in some areas (like medical decisions) while having help in others", "Only if the lawyer says so", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Legal status can be "plenary" (all) or "limited." The specific terms of the guardianship determine the person''s rights.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with "Severe Anxiety" is so panicked about a surgery that they cannot focus on the explanation. The physician should:', '["Wait until they calm down, or use a mild anxiolytic (with surrogate consent if needed) to facilitate a calm discussion and assessment", "Force them to sign the form", "Assume they lack capacity and ask the spouse", "None of the above"]', 0, 'Acute panic can cause temporary incapacity. Reducing the panic is a therapeutic step toward restoring the patient''s ability to decide.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When documenting a capacity assessment, the physician should specifically record:', '["The patient''s answers to questions about the risks/benefits", "The logic the patient used to arrive at their decision", "The clinical reason why capacity was questioned in the first place", "All of the above"]', 3, 'Detailed documentation is essential for both clinical continuity and legal protection.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient says: "I understand the risks, I just don''t care if I die." If this is NOT due to clinical depression, is it a lack of capacity?', '["Yes, not caring is a lack of reasoning", "No, ''Value-based indifference'' is a choice a competent person can make (e.g., in extreme old age or chronic illness)", "Only if they are in the ICU", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Indifference or acceptance of death is not synonymous with incapacity, provided the patient understands the facts.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is the most important "Environmental" factor in a capacity assessment?', '["The color of the room", "Ensuring a quiet, private setting where the patient feels safe and is not being interrupted", "The doctor''s tie", "The time of the news"]', 1, 'Optimizing the setting allows the patient to perform at their cognitive best.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with "Broca''s Aphasia" can understand everything but cannot speak. The best way to assess their "Reasoning" is to:', '["Assume they can''t reason", "Ask ''Yes/No'' questions that require logical steps (e.g., ''If you don''t have the surgery, will you be able to walk?'')", "Wait for them to heal", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Structured "Yes/No" or "Multiple Choice" questions can probe reasoning even without verbal fluency.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'If a patient is "Incompetent" but becomes "Capacitated" during the hospital stay, what happens to their surrogate''s authority?', '["It continues until discharge", "It ends immediately; the patient resumes their own decision-making authority", "It is up to the doctor", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Surrogacy is a fallback. As soon as the patient can speak for themselves, they must be the one to decide.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with capacity asks their physician to make the decision for them. The physician should:', '["Refuse, that''s paternalism", "Accept the ''Delegated Autonomy'' after ensuring the patient understands the risks and still wants the doctor to choose", "Always choose the most expensive option", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Delegated autonomy is a valid exercise of a patient''s right to decide *how* they want to be treated, including choosing to trust their doctor''s judgment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A "Neuropsychological Evaluation" is most helpful in capacity assessment when:', '["The patient is annoying", "There are subtle cognitive deficits (e.g., early dementia or TBI) that make it difficult to determine if the patient truly understands the nuances of the decision", "The insurance pays for it", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Neuropsychologists can provide detailed mapping of cognitive strengths and weaknesses relevant to decision-making.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient "Understands" the procedure but cannot "Retain" the information for more than 5 minutes. Do they have capacity for a complex decision?', '["Yes, they understood it once", "No, because meaningful reasoning requires the ability to hold the information in memory long enough to process it", "Only if they have a notes page", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Retention (at least long enough for the decision process) is a component of the "Understanding" element.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is true regarding "Capacity for Marriage" vs. "Capacity for Medical Decisions"?', '["They are the same", "They have different thresholds; a person may have the capacity to choose a spouse but not the capacity to choose a complex surgery", "Marriage requires higher capacity", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Different decisions have different "Thresholds." Legal standards vary, but the clinical principle is the same.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A physician is assessing a patient''s "Appreciation." They should ask:', '["What is your name?", "What do you think will happen to *you* if you don''t have this treatment?", "Can you repeat what I just said?", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Appreciation is about the personal relevance of the medical facts.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with "Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder" (OCD) is so stuck on a minor risk that they cannot move forward with a life-saving surgery. Is this a lack of capacity?', '["No, it''s just a personality trait", "Yes, if the OCD is functionally preventing the patient from ''Reasoning'' through the total clinical picture", "Only if they wash their hands", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Extreme ritualization or fixation (symptoms of OCD) can impair the reasoning process for a specific decision.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When an "Ethics Committee" is called for a capacity case, their role is to:', '["Make the final decision", "Provide a recommendation and facilitate communication between the stakeholders", "Fire the doctor", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Ethics committees are advisory, not judicial.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A "Medical Directive" that was signed 20 years ago conflicts with the current reports of the patient''s family. The physician should:', '["Always follow the document", "Try to determine if the document still reflects the patient''s most recent values and if any significant life changes would have altered their wishes", "Ignore the document", "None of the above"]', 1, 'While legal documents are strong evidence, the goal is to follow the patient''s *current* (or last known) wishes.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient has capacity but is "Pressured" by their physician into a decision. This is a violation of:', '["Veracity", "Voluntariness (an element of informed consent)", "Justice", "None of the above"]', 1, 'The physician must facilitate a choice, not dictate it.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The "Golden Rule" of capacity assessment is:', '["The doctor is always right", "Maximize the patient''s participation in their own care to the greatest extent possible, given their cognitive abilities", "Ask the spouse first", "None of the above"]', 1, 'Respect for persons requires that we honor whatever level of autonomy the patient is capable of exercising.');
